diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** OSPF control plane across the core layer (csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2, and r1).

**Mechanism and what we observed:** A topology-wide reachability probe showed zero packet loss on all host-to-host paths. Direct `show ip ospf neighbor` on csw1, csw2, dswb2, and r1 confirmed every expected adjacency is in **Full** state: csw1 holds 6 Full neighbors (r1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2), csw2 likewise, and r1 sees both core switches as Full/DR. The ospf_snapshot helper initially appeared to flag missing adjacencies on csw1.eth6 and csw2.eth6, but this was a parsing artefact in the per-interface display — the live OSPF process disagreed entirely.

**Localization:** No single device or link is implicated. FRR daemons on all routers show a single clean start at 01:08 with no restarts. All OSPF up-times are ~5 minutes, consistent with normal lab boot, not with adjacency cycling.

**Ruling out intermittent flapping:** No flap script or process was found on any device. Carrier-change counters on r1.eth0, r1.eth1, and csw1.eth0 are all at 2 — the startup-only baseline for Kathara veth pairs. No OSPF or syslog entries recording adjacency state transitions were found. No excess errors or dropped packets appear on any interface.

**Root cause:** The helpdesk alert is unconfirmed. There is no active OSPF flapping, no faulty link, and no misconfigured device — the OSPF core fabric is fully stable.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. Monitor OSPF neighbor up-times over a longer window; if alerts recur, correlate with a tc-based fault (netem jitter) or a timer misconfiguration that only manifests under load.
=== END ===